caught\\n2. Make all commands handle `ConfigParseError` with user-friendly messages (currently only `lint.rs` does this)\\n\\nThe structs that need `deny_unknown_fields`:\\n- `BivvyConfig` - the root struct (line 11)\\n- `Settings` - global settings (line 51)\\n- `StepConfig` - step definitions (line 166)\\n- `WorkflowConfig` - workflow definitions (line 371)\\n\\n\\n- `StepOverride` (line 400)\\n- `WorkflowSettings` (line 417)\\n- `EnvironmentConfig` (line 570)\\n- `StepEnvironmentOverride` (line 603)\\n\\nThough I need to be careful with structs using `#[serde(tag = \\\"type\\\")]` or `#[serde(untagged)]` since those might conflict with `deny_unknown_fields`. Actually, I should focus on just the root `BivvyConfig` struct first rather than applying this everywhere at once, since adding it to all nested structs could introduce unexpected breakage.\\n\\nThe real value is catching typos at the top level like `stesp` instead of `steps`. For nested structs like `StepConfig` and `Settings`, I'd want to be more selective. Let me check if `StepConfig` uses any hash maps or catch-all patterns that would conflict with strict field validation.\\n\\nI'm also thinking about how the merger works\u2014it loads configs as raw YAML values, merges them, then converts to `BivvyConfig`, so `deny_unknown_fields` would only apply at that final conversion step.\\n\\nNow I'm going to follow a test-driven approach: write tests first to verify unknown fields produce parse errors and that commands display user-friendly error messages, then implement the fix. I'll add these tests to the loader module.\\n\\nThe core implementation involves adding `deny_unknown_fields` to the main config structs like `BivvyConfig`, `StepConfig`, and `Settings`, then updating all commands to handle `ConfigParseError` properly instead of just the lint command. I need to be careful with HashMap value types since the HashMap keys themselves aren't treated as struct fields.\\n\\nLet me start implementing...\\n\\nActually, I should follow the test-first approach from the workflow\u2014write the tests, verify they fail, then implement the fix.\",\"signature\":\"\"}],\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":1,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":1289,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":47047,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":1289,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":0},\"output_tokens\":8,\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"inference_geo\":\"not_available\"},\"context_management\":null},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"71626d50-8e30-42b0-86de-6cafbf80e23f\",\"uuid\":\"13c0607b-acbb-4e05-b82c-65209d1724ca\"}\n{\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-opus-4-6\",\"id\":\"msg_01DSayJaP2UhcsqxXEx6xtEn\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"I now have a clear picture of the bug. The root cause is in `src/config/schema.rs`: `BivvyConfig` and its nested structs use `#[serde(default)]` and serde silently ignores unknown fields. So a config with typos (e.g., `stesp:` instead of `steps:`) parses successfully into a default config with no warning.\\n\\nThe fix has two parts:\\n1. Add `#[serde(deny_unknown_fields)]` to config structs so typos produce parse errors\\n2. Handle `ConfigParseError` in all commands (currently only `lint` handles it)\\n\\nFollowing the TDD workflow \u2014 tests first.\"}],\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":1,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":1289,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":47047,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":1289,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":0},\"output_tokens\":8,\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"inference_geo\":\"not_available\"},\"context_management\":null},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"71626d50-8e30-42b0-86de-6cafbf80e23f\",\"uuid\":\"ee959aa5-4ef4-42c7-914e-ed613986e81a\"}\n{\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-opus-4-6\",\"id\":\"msg_01DSayJaP2UhcsqxXEx6xtEn\",\"type\":\"mess